the target language is: prolog.

% Relatives: two people are relatives when they descend from the same
% root of the family tree. Every rule below asks "who are the ancestors of
% X?" or "who descends from the root?" — the relatives of a person, the
% members of the family, how many relatives each has — and the same call,
% with the same person, comes up in several rules of one query and many
% times over in a query about everybody.
%
% `; memorable` on the ancestor template makes the reasoner compute each
% distinct call once per query and replay its answers, explanation
% included, wherever that call recurs (docs/user/reference/language.md
% §2.4). The answers are the same with or without the marker; so are the
% explanations: a replayed call shows the same sub-proof the first one did.
%
% Calls are cached by their shape: "which ancestor is an ancestor of
% Lettice" (first place free, second bound) and "Owen is an ancestor of
% which person" (the reverse) are two calls, each computed once; "is Owen
% an ancestor of Lettice" (both bound) would be a third.

the templates are:
    *a person* is a parent of *a child*.
    *an ancestor* is an ancestor of *a person*; memorable.
    *a person* is a relative of *an other person*.
    *a person* is a family member.
    *a person* has *a number* relatives.

the knowledge base relatives includes:

an ancestor is an ancestor of a person if
    the ancestor is a parent of the person.

an ancestor is an ancestor of a person if
    a parent is a parent of the person
    and the ancestor is an ancestor of the parent.

a person is a relative of an other person if
    a root is an ancestor of the person
    and it is not the case that
        a parent is a parent of the root
    and the root is an ancestor of the other person
    and the person is different from the other person.

a person is a family member if
    a root is an ancestor of the person
    and it is not the case that
        a parent is a parent of the root.

a person has a number relatives if
    the person is a family member
    and the number is the count of each relative such that
        the person is a relative of the relative.

scenario tudors is:
    Owen is a parent of Henry.
    Henry is a parent of Mary.
    Henry is a parent of Elizabeth.
    Henry is a parent of Edward.
    Mary is a parent of Anne.
    Elizabeth is a parent of Robert.
    Robert is a parent of Lettice.
    Robert is a parent of Penelope.
    Edward is a parent of Jane.
    ancestors expects answers ["Robert is an ancestor of Lettice", "Elizabeth is an ancestor of Lettice", "Henry is an ancestor of Lettice", "Owen is an ancestor of Lettice"].
    relatives expects answers ["Lettice is a relative of Henry", "Lettice is a relative of Mary", "Lettice is a relative of Elizabeth", "Lettice is a relative of Edward", "Lettice is a relative of Anne", "Lettice is a relative of Robert", "Lettice is a relative of Penelope", "Lettice is a relative of Jane"].
    count expects answers ["Lettice has 8 relatives"].
    everybody expects answers ["Henry has 8 relatives", "Mary has 8 relatives", "Elizabeth has 8 relatives", "Edward has 8 relatives", "Anne has 8 relatives", "Robert has 8 relatives", "Lettice has 8 relatives", "Penelope has 8 relatives", "Jane has 8 relatives"].

query ancestors is:
    which ancestor is an ancestor of Lettice.

query relatives is:
    Lettice is a relative of which person.

query count is:
    Lettice has which number relatives.

query everybody is:
    which person has which number relatives.
